ompany because  a company has an obligation David to  actually create a safe and healthful  workplace and if they don't do so and  that company can be liable so it's  always a good thing to notify the  company right away if you think that you  know something has happened in the  workplace that you think just you know  isn't quite right can you talk a little  bit about how power dynamics influences  how a lot of these incidents are dealt  with by employers because we can all  imagine a situation where if if there is  a c-level executive for example or  someone that that is sort of above human  resources in the organizational chart  who is maybe engaging in some of this  behavior we can all imagine how going to  Human Resources is not necessarily going  to be an experience where Human  Resources will be an unbiased arbiter of  what took place if they also are subject  to that those exact same power dynamics  so can you address how that might be  managed in some way well first off to  begin with that's a bad system I mean  anytime Human Resources is reporting in  to a person or persons that they then  have to investigate I mean that that's  just a recipe for for impotence and you  know in in with societies like the site  Human Resource Management other employer  organizations they have systems that  they recommend that employers Institute  where you have you can report for  instance outside Human Resources or  maybe anonymously to the board directly  when you you feel as if Human Resources  is influenced inappropriately by you  know someone above them that might be  involved in in the harassment but I  think a generally that the first place  to go is Human Resources and the reason  I say that David is that that the law  says  our Supreme Court has said that when an  employee notifies his or her human  resources department and does so  promptly and does so in a specific way  the law then will protect them the key  though is is to make sure people in the  company are aware of what's going on and  doing so as soon as you possibly can we  know from experience that there is a  degree of stigma in society about coming  forward and saying I was a victim of X Y  or Z can you talk a little bit about  from a legal standpoint how statutes of  limitations can actually create an issue  when there is a delay in coming forward  but also about ways in which it doesn't  matter and wit in which claims are just  as valid later than they would be  immediately after an alleged incident  you know III think it's really important  because there is this stigma I think out  there the sense that you know if I'm  coming forward I necessarily am a victim  I'm the one who should be blamed and I  think it should be just the opposite you  know that the reality is that in the  employment space in particular we we all  ought to be on equal footing and  certainly we all ought to expect from  our employers that employer is going to  create that safe space for us I mean  that's the law requires that and so  there there is nothing wrong about  coming forward and I think it's an  employer's obligation to make that very  clear to its employees so how does an  employer do that I think the best way is  really in training you know the  employers that routinely train their  employees and I'm not talking about just  some sort of online module where you go  and you know you check some boxes but  real training where you bring employees  in and you explain to them the  importance of coming forward and  notifying the company so the company can  do something about that actors and  create that that safe space and to make  it very clear to employees that  that the company will take any complaint  seriously we'll move quickly to  investigate and we'll take prompt  corrective action will potentially  remove that employee that's been a  sexual harasser as an example from the  from the workspace so really is the  company's obligation to remove that  stigma to to make employees feel as if  if they come forward they're going to be  lauded and they're gonna be listened to  and and not retaliated against so that  that certainly tells us about how the  employer can manage circumstances when  they're sort of have been allegations or  to create an environment where people  are comfortable coming forward and  saying hey here's what happened I got a  call from one of our viewers last week  saying something along the lines of hey  how can employers prevent sexual  harassment in the first place and I  think that that's difficult I mean my  reaction was if by the time you're an  adult and you get hired somewhere you  don't yet know that it's not okay to do  these things I don't know how much an  employer can really do can you talk  about that a little bit yeah so the  first thing is is to make sure that the  people you let in the door know those  things you know background checks  careful review of references you know  what we're seeing I think in each one of  these instances in in the media is that  these are not one-off situations these  are men that seem to do this over and  over and over again and so they develop  a reputation and if an employer does its  job in vetting employees at the outset  especially senior people that they're  bringing in to make sure that they are  not individuals who have a you know a  track record for doing these types of  things I think that's critical how does  it do that though because presumably I  mean I'm just thinking through like okay  you check references very carefully you  can't necessarily go and talk to people  that were managed by your applicant  right because more than likely their  references are either colleagues or  people even senior to them or in c-level  cases maybe even boards of directors  how can you say how can you get access  to I guess the people who might actually  know or be willing to talk about that  prior track record so I think the first  place is those previous employers and  what we're seeing 